base_view: neondb_telemetry__attempt
label: Attempt scored (dev-A)
description: >-
  Dev-A accuracy, failure and cost analysis. Attempts joined to both frozen
  scorers, the public question, the run, the latest labels and the
  attempt_scored view (scorer-disagreement flip rate). Defaults to
  partition dev-a because only dev-A attempts carry scores.
fields:
- neondb_telemetry__attempt.*
- neondb_telemetry__score.*
- neondb_telemetry__question.*
- neondb_telemetry__run.*
- neondb_telemetry__attempt_label_latest.*
- neondb_telemetry__attempt_scored.*
- neondb_telemetry__dev_a_frame.*
joins:
  neondb_telemetry__score: {}
  neondb_telemetry__question: {}
  neondb_telemetry__run: {}
  neondb_telemetry__attempt_label_latest: {}
  neondb_telemetry__attempt_scored: {}
  neondb_telemetry__dev_a_frame: {}
default_filters:
  neondb_telemetry__attempt.partition:
    is: dev-a
ai_context: >-
  Report official_accuracy and sensitivity_accuracy together; never pick one.
  Group by neondb_telemetry__attempt.arm, not run.arm, because multi-condition
  runs have a NULL run arm. When comparing accuracy across arms, filter
  neondb_telemetry__dev_a_frame.in_official_frame to true (or
  in_sensitivity_frame for the sensitivity scorer). Without that filter each
  arm gets its own denominator and the arms are not comparable, because the
  arms did not all run the same questions. The frames are 122 questions
  official and 121 sensitivity. A NULL outcome means the scorer could not
  evaluate the question at all, because the benchmark's own reference query
  fails against the pinned public databases or its result exceeds the
  sensitivity row cap; it is a benchmark-input defect, never a failure of the
  evaluated system, and such rows are excluded from every denominator.
  neondb_telemetry__attempt_scored.cost_per_official_correct is the cost of
  official-scored attempts per correct answer; it is NULL for arms without
  priced cost (C4, C5, E02). Cost for those arms is structurally unavailable
  because the Omni AI job API reports no cost or credit figure per job. Never
  estimate it from token counts: the arms bill through a different provider and
  the records carry no cache breakdown, so a token rate card misprices even the
  arms it is fitted on.
